WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.169 --> 00:00:07.629
Speak up.

00:00:08.713 --> 00:00:11.132
Bring your whole selves to work.

00:00:12.175 --> 00:00:15.679
Be the difference that you want to see.

00:00:16.888 --> 00:00:18.056
Sound familiar?

00:00:19.391 --> 00:00:23.061
Started to sound very familiar
to quite a few employees.

00:00:24.104 --> 00:00:29.276
Now many leaders are asking voices
of difference to speak up.

00:00:30.193 --> 00:00:36.116
And that's because what gets said,
and what doesn't, in our workplaces

00:00:36.157 --> 00:00:41.246
has a huge consequence for things
like ethical conduct,

00:00:41.288 --> 00:00:45.125
innovation, inclusion, talent retention.

00:00:46.876 --> 00:00:50.922
So more and more employees at the moment
are starting to speak up

00:00:50.922 --> 00:00:53.717
about social and environmental concerns.

00:00:54.509 --> 00:00:59.097
And this is great, but it's not always
going quite to plan for everybody.

00:01:00.015 --> 00:01:02.642
A finance director I've been working with,

00:01:02.684 --> 00:01:06.563
he's been asking his employees
to speak up for quite a while,

00:01:06.605 --> 00:01:08.315
and now they've started to.

00:01:08.982 --> 00:01:12.819
So they're saying,
OK, let's talk about race.

00:01:13.653 --> 00:01:15.905
Let's talk about gender equity.

00:01:16.656 --> 00:01:18.867
Climate change, I want to talk about that.

00:01:18.908 --> 00:01:20.660
Modern slavery.

00:01:21.995 --> 00:01:26.207
And this finance director came to me
somewhat stunned and said,

00:01:26.249 --> 00:01:31.421
"You know, Megan, I've got to admit,
when I asked people to speak up,

00:01:31.421 --> 00:01:36.343
I was kind of thinking that I'd get more
transparency around compliance issues

00:01:36.384 --> 00:01:38.053
and a few good ideas.

00:01:39.137 --> 00:01:42.432
I didn't really bank
on getting everything else."

00:01:43.892 --> 00:01:47.187
But this is an era of employee activism

00:01:47.228 --> 00:01:48.772
and that's great,

00:01:48.813 --> 00:01:52.776
but why does it end up
on the front pages of the newspaper

00:01:52.817 --> 00:01:55.445
for the wrong reasons sometimes?

00:01:55.737 --> 00:01:59.866
Employees walking out, getting fired,

00:01:59.908 --> 00:02:01.826
taking to social media.

00:02:02.535 --> 00:02:05.789
Organizational reputations destroyed

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:09.459
or investors seeking change
at the top of organizations.

00:02:11.753 --> 00:02:15.548
So my research over the past
few years with John Higgins

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:22.389
has involved interviewing hundreds
of activists and leaders

00:02:22.389 --> 00:02:25.058
and activist leaders.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:30.647
And our work’s in service of enabling
voices of difference

00:02:30.647 --> 00:02:33.316
to make a difference in the workplace.

00:02:34.734 --> 00:02:39.030
Now today, I'm just going
to draw out four key findings,

00:02:39.072 --> 00:02:41.741
and I'm also going to go through
a few dos and don'ts

00:02:41.783 --> 00:02:47.956
for leaders who want to navigate
this territory proactively, productively.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.000
So let me start with a question.

00:02:51.501 --> 00:02:56.131
When I say the word "activist,"

00:02:56.172 --> 00:02:57.590
what comes to mind?

00:02:59.092 --> 00:03:03.722
What images, what thoughts,
what assumptions?

00:03:04.931 --> 00:03:08.685
Well, we've asked thousands
of people that question,

00:03:08.727 --> 00:03:13.106
and I can safely say
that the words "activist" and "activism"

00:03:13.106 --> 00:03:15.191
are loaded terms.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.195
They mean everything from progress

00:03:19.237 --> 00:03:22.949
and courage and passion and change

00:03:22.991 --> 00:03:28.413
through to protest, rebellion, violence.

00:03:29.622 --> 00:03:35.295
It's quite cool to be labeled an activist
in some parts of the world

00:03:35.336 --> 00:03:37.380
and in relation to some issues.

00:03:37.922 --> 00:03:41.843
And in other parts of the world
and in relation to other issues,

00:03:41.885 --> 00:03:45.805
being labelled an activist
is life-threatening.

00:03:47.932 --> 00:03:51.644
So we need to understand the assumptions

00:03:51.686 --> 00:03:55.231
and the associations
that we bring to activism

00:03:55.273 --> 00:03:58.818
because of course that affects
how we respond to it.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:03.782
I was working with the board
of a health care organization.

00:04:03.823 --> 00:04:06.034
And in the coffee break,

00:04:06.075 --> 00:04:11.122
they started to talk about an employee
who'd been pretty vocal

00:04:11.164 --> 00:04:15.418
on the internal comms channels
about climate change,

00:04:15.418 --> 00:04:19.631
and he was quite critical
of what the organization had been doing.

00:04:20.632 --> 00:04:23.885
It was really interesting,
because some of the executives

00:04:23.885 --> 00:04:26.638
labeled him as a troublemaker,

00:04:26.638 --> 00:04:28.848
kind of wanted to get rid of him.

00:04:29.432 --> 00:04:34.604
But there were a few executives
that saw him as a trailblazer,

00:04:34.604 --> 00:04:35.814
and actually a couple

00:04:35.855 --> 00:04:39.150
that wanted to invite him
into the board to educate them.

00:04:39.692 --> 00:04:40.819
OK?

00:04:41.569 --> 00:04:45.198
So we've got to the first
key point for our leaders

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:50.453
is to understand that activism
is in the eye of the beholder,

00:04:50.495 --> 00:04:54.874
as Ruchika Tulshyan, an author
and activist, told us:

00:04:54.916 --> 00:05:00.797
“What looks like rebellion to you
is another person’s basic human rights.”

00:05:00.839 --> 00:05:05.426
So the first thing you've got to do
is really become aware

00:05:05.426 --> 00:05:08.888
of the kind of assumptions and judgments

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:13.977
that you and your colleagues
bring to activism

00:05:14.018 --> 00:05:16.521
in order that you can then respond

00:05:16.521 --> 00:05:19.607
with more awareness and more mindfulness.

00:05:21.317 --> 00:05:23.069
Second point,

00:05:23.111 --> 00:05:27.323
leaders can find themselves
in an optimism bubble,

00:05:27.365 --> 00:05:29.742
we sometimes call a “delusion bubble.”

00:05:30.618 --> 00:05:33.371
As you get more senior,

00:05:33.371 --> 00:05:38.710
you overestimate the degree
to which other people are speaking up.

00:05:40.044 --> 00:05:44.465
You overestimate your approachability,

00:05:44.465 --> 00:05:47.635
and you overestimate
your listening skills.

00:05:48.511 --> 00:05:52.974
And that all means that you underestimate

00:05:53.016 --> 00:05:56.603
the strength of feeling that might exist
with some of your employees.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:58.771
Now, one of the key reasons for this

00:05:58.813 --> 00:06:01.691
is something we call
an “advantage blindness.”

00:06:02.442 --> 00:06:08.281
So when we have the labels and titles
that convey status and authority

00:06:08.323 --> 00:06:13.161
in a particular context
like hierarchy, for example,

00:06:13.202 --> 00:06:19.042
we're often the last person to realize
the impact that those labels have

00:06:19.083 --> 00:06:22.670
on how other people are able
to speak up to us.

00:06:22.712 --> 00:06:26.466
In fact, it's not until
we don't have those labels

00:06:26.466 --> 00:06:28.509
that we can kind of look at them and go,

00:06:28.551 --> 00:06:33.723
"Gosh, they make a difference
to how people can voice around here."

00:06:35.391 --> 00:06:40.855
So this point for leaders
is all about understanding,

00:06:40.855 --> 00:06:44.025
you know, are you in one
of these optimism bubbles?

00:06:44.067 --> 00:06:45.735
Are you a bit detached?

00:06:45.777 --> 00:06:50.073
How do you know what your employees

00:06:50.114 --> 00:06:52.659
find matters in their organizations?

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:54.160
Do you? How?

00:06:54.911 --> 00:06:58.247
I was talking to the head
of a retail organization,

00:06:58.247 --> 00:07:00.375
and she was saying
that her leadership team

00:07:00.375 --> 00:07:04.671
spend a lot of time in stores, listening.

00:07:05.254 --> 00:07:07.966
And she said something
I thought was really interesting.

00:07:07.966 --> 00:07:09.133
She said,

00:07:09.175 --> 00:07:13.429
"You can't delegate your listening
responsibility to pulse surveys."

00:07:15.098 --> 00:07:18.309
You've got to show up
with your ears wide open.

00:07:18.726 --> 00:07:22.897
So what this means is,
don't assume you know what matters.

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.442
You know, sharpen your antennae.

00:07:26.442 --> 00:07:27.610
Try and figure out.

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.405
We've written about lots of ways
that you can do that.

00:07:30.446 --> 00:07:32.740
But underlying all of those methods

00:07:32.782 --> 00:07:37.620
is an understanding that it’s almost
inevitable that you’re detached a bit,

00:07:37.662 --> 00:07:40.123
and you need to do
a lot more work, actually,

00:07:40.164 --> 00:07:42.917
to really find out
what matters to employees.

00:07:44.544 --> 00:07:46.462
So third point,

00:07:46.504 --> 00:07:50.842
inaction is as political as action.

00:07:50.883 --> 00:07:56.264
We’ve met quite a few leaders that say
that they’re neutral on certain issues

00:07:56.264 --> 00:07:58.224
or apolitical.

00:07:59.434 --> 00:08:01.102
There's no such thing.

00:08:02.937 --> 00:08:09.861
Inaction on things like climate change
is as political as action.

00:08:11.112 --> 00:08:16.576
I was working with an HR director
in the construction industry

00:08:16.576 --> 00:08:21.289
right at the moment
where a competitor had said

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:25.043
some fairly disparaging things
about women in the industry.

00:08:25.793 --> 00:08:27.587
It's a huge controversy.

00:08:27.628 --> 00:08:31.799
And this HR director really
didn't want to get involved.

00:08:32.216 --> 00:08:36.471
He just wanted to avoid
the conflict, stay out of it.

00:08:36.471 --> 00:08:38.639
But his employees wouldn’t let him

00:08:38.681 --> 00:08:43.811
because his silence would have
communicated complicity.

00:08:44.896 --> 00:08:47.523
Now what I am not saying,

00:08:47.523 --> 00:08:49.817
even though I am often
accused of saying it,

00:08:49.859 --> 00:08:53.362
what I am not saying
is that therefore you need to act

00:08:53.404 --> 00:08:55.740
on every issue that's out there.

00:08:56.282 --> 00:08:57.450
Of course you don't.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.911
And of course you can't, it's infeasible.

00:08:59.952 --> 00:09:01.746
What I am saying,

00:09:01.788 --> 00:09:07.085
as a leader, is that you need
to make conscious, coherent,

00:09:07.126 --> 00:09:10.004
authentic choices

00:09:10.004 --> 00:09:14.300
about what you will make a stand on
and what you won't.

00:09:15.176 --> 00:09:18.346
And do that in conjunction
with your stakeholders and, of course,

00:09:18.346 --> 00:09:21.307
your employees are one
of your key stakeholders there.

00:09:23.226 --> 00:09:27.396
Final point is that it's
useful to understand

00:09:27.438 --> 00:09:32.110
what your employees think your
response has been

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:34.779
to activist issues so far.

00:09:35.738 --> 00:09:36.948
Not what you think it is,

00:09:36.989 --> 00:09:39.283
but what do your employees
think it's been?

00:09:39.826 --> 00:09:43.287
And in our research, we came up
with a kind of taxonomy

00:09:43.329 --> 00:09:45.998
of different leadership responses.

00:09:45.998 --> 00:09:48.960
It starts with nonexistent

00:09:49.001 --> 00:09:50.920
or, "Activism?

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:52.130
What activism?"

00:09:53.131 --> 00:09:57.385
We talked to a chief executive
in the manufacturing industry.

00:09:58.136 --> 00:10:01.597
And midway through our conversation,
I asked him about climate change

00:10:01.597 --> 00:10:05.434
and his strategy and stance
on environmental issues.

00:10:05.476 --> 00:10:08.729
And he looked at me utterly baffled.

00:10:09.897 --> 00:10:12.400
It was nowhere on the agenda.

00:10:13.192 --> 00:10:16.529
Now, that looks increasingly
inconceivable, actually,

00:10:16.571 --> 00:10:18.197
but it certainly still happens.

00:10:18.948 --> 00:10:20.867
And then you get suppression.

00:10:21.742 --> 00:10:26.330
Or, "Let's just expel those voices
before it spreads."

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:33.671
Now this is where leaders
explicitly silence or implicitly,

00:10:33.713 --> 00:10:36.507
because employees know
that if they do speak up,

00:10:36.549 --> 00:10:39.177
it will probably cost them
their next promotion.

00:10:39.886 --> 00:10:43.806
Or indeed, if they do speak up,
they might be ignored.

00:10:43.848 --> 00:10:48.895
We surveyed just over 3,000 employees
in a recent project,

00:10:48.936 --> 00:10:52.398
and just over one in five employees

00:10:52.398 --> 00:10:55.902
expect to be ignored if they speak up

00:10:55.902 --> 00:10:58.821
about wider social
and environmental concerns.

00:11:00.281 --> 00:11:04.327
After that comes something
that we call "facadism"

00:11:04.327 --> 00:11:07.496
or, "Let's just say the right things."

00:11:08.039 --> 00:11:11.792
This is when leaders make
proclamations about what’s important,

00:11:11.792 --> 00:11:15.171
and they may even say
what they're going to do about it,

00:11:15.213 --> 00:11:17.215
but nothing happens.

00:11:17.965 --> 00:11:20.801
In the wake of George Floyd's murder,

00:11:20.801 --> 00:11:24.263
there were many organizations
that made statements of support

00:11:24.305 --> 00:11:26.599
for the Black Lives Matter movement.

00:11:28.309 --> 00:11:32.730
When the American Marketing Association
investigated things shortly after,

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:36.901
they found that less than one in 10
had made any concrete changes.

00:11:39.153 --> 00:11:43.199
Then you get to something
we call defensive engagement,

00:11:43.199 --> 00:11:46.452
or, "Let's just do
what the lawyers tell us."

00:11:47.453 --> 00:11:51.415
Now, this is where leaders
do engage on a topic,

00:11:52.291 --> 00:11:54.543
but only because they really have to.

00:11:55.628 --> 00:11:59.757
Again, working with a senior team recently
in the farmer industry,

00:11:59.799 --> 00:12:03.427
the issue of diversity and inclusion
came up on the agenda.

00:12:03.469 --> 00:12:06.389
It was dealt with in about five minutes.

00:12:07.014 --> 00:12:08.266
And essentially they said,

00:12:08.307 --> 00:12:11.435
"Let's send everybody on a course
and count the number of women."

00:12:11.811 --> 00:12:14.355
That was kind of as far as it got.

00:12:14.355 --> 00:12:16.107
They did the bare minimum.

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:22.989
And then there's a step change
to what we call dialogic engagement

00:12:22.989 --> 00:12:26.951
or, "Let's sit down, listen and learn."

00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:30.288
And the reason why it's a step change

00:12:30.288 --> 00:12:35.167
is because leaders here know
that they don’t know the answer,

00:12:35.167 --> 00:12:38.504
and they really want to find out
what they don't know.

00:12:39.422 --> 00:12:40.589
OK?

00:12:40.631 --> 00:12:43.592
So we talked to an entrepreneur

00:12:43.634 --> 00:12:48.806
who had taken over ex-UK
car manufacturing plant.

00:12:50.099 --> 00:12:54.395
And the workers there were very upset
about working conditions.

00:12:54.812 --> 00:12:59.984
And so this entrepreneur decided in,
General McChrystal's terms,

00:12:59.984 --> 00:13:03.321
to share information
until it was almost illegal.

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:05.197
In other words,

00:13:05.239 --> 00:13:08.576
he'd gotten the employees
and opened up the books,

00:13:08.617 --> 00:13:12.663
shared information and shared
decision making with them

00:13:12.705 --> 00:13:14.415
about what they needed to do.

00:13:14.749 --> 00:13:18.753
And that was a vastly different
leadership style

00:13:18.794 --> 00:13:20.796
from the ones that they've been used to.

00:13:21.756 --> 00:13:25.176
Now right at the end,
we've got stimulating activism.

00:13:25.176 --> 00:13:28.804
This is when leaders say,
"Let's be the activist."

00:13:29.847 --> 00:13:33.392
This is the Ben and Jerry's
and the Patagonias of the world.

00:13:33.434 --> 00:13:35.978
And they recruit activists.

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:37.646
They promote activists.

00:13:37.688 --> 00:13:42.401
They keep hold of activists
in their organizations.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:47.740
Now, there's many things that I could
take out from this taxonomy.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:50.368
Let me draw just two
key learnings out here.

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:52.078
First of all,

00:13:52.119 --> 00:13:58.084
you need to know where your employees
think your response has been so far,

00:13:58.125 --> 00:14:00.169
not where you think it's been.

00:14:00.169 --> 00:14:01.337
Because guess what?

00:14:01.337 --> 00:14:03.464
Let's go back to that optimism bubble.

00:14:03.923 --> 00:14:06.092
The more senior you are,

00:14:06.092 --> 00:14:09.512
the more likely you are to think
that you're in dialogue.

00:14:11.013 --> 00:14:13.182
But if I ask a more junior employee,

00:14:13.224 --> 00:14:16.185
they're more likely to say,
"No, that's a facade."

00:14:16.519 --> 00:14:19.230
Or even actually,
"I'm scared to speak up."

00:14:20.815 --> 00:14:25.403
And the second key point
is dialogue is messy.

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:29.782
It's jam-packed full of vulnerability,

00:14:29.824 --> 00:14:32.910
ambiguity, disagreement.

00:14:33.327 --> 00:14:36.831
That's why leaders try
and avoid it so much.

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:41.502
But you can't avoid it any longer,
that's not a sustainable strategy.

00:14:41.544 --> 00:14:46.382
So we need to get far better
at experimenting,

00:14:46.424 --> 00:14:49.635
at expecting fallout,

00:14:49.635 --> 00:14:52.012
about learning from mistakes.

00:14:54.598 --> 00:15:00.104
So in summary, we are entering
an age of employee activism.

00:15:01.439 --> 00:15:07.945
And if we can't or won't hear voices
of difference in our organization,

00:15:07.987 --> 00:15:12.908
we need to consider that
like the canary in the coal mine.

00:15:13.451 --> 00:15:16.328
In other words, a signal of danger.

00:15:17.746 --> 00:15:22.334
Because if we can't talk
about stuff that matters to us,

00:15:22.334 --> 00:15:25.504
but that we differ on,

00:15:25.546 --> 00:15:29.216
that spells disaster in our organizations.

00:15:29.258 --> 00:15:32.803
For things like ethical conduct,
innovation, inclusion,

00:15:32.845 --> 00:15:34.722
talent retention, performance.

00:15:36.974 --> 00:15:43.314
So maybe in the face of some of these
enormous social and environmental issues,

00:15:43.314 --> 00:15:50.112
maybe we're finally starting to reassess
what good leadership looks like.

00:15:51.822 --> 00:15:56.410
Maybe we're starting to see
leadership as activism.

00:15:57.745 --> 00:15:59.079
And in doing that,

00:15:59.079 --> 00:16:02.416
maybe we'll enable voices of difference

00:16:02.416 --> 00:16:05.085
to make a difference in the workplace

00:16:05.085 --> 00:16:07.963
by allowing them to speak truth to power.

00:16:08.881 --> 00:16:10.090
Thank you.

